<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lichfield Gospels</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St Chad Gospels, Book of Saint Chad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> are an 8th-century illuminated gospel book in the Insular style, kept at Lichfield Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In the 9th century it was at Llandeilo Fawr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and notes written in its margins there include some of the earliest surviving written Welsh. It reached Lichfield by the late 10th century and is also known as the St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> Gospels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lichfield-gospels.jpg" alt="Lichfield Gospels" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_St_Luke,_St_Chad_Gospels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
